    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "First, we know that the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the two numbers is 6. So, let's express the two numbers as $6m$ and $6n$, where $m$ and $n$ are relatively prime (i.e., $gcd(m, n) = 1$).\n\nThe least common multiple (LCM) of the two numbers is 36. The formula for the LCM in terms of $m$ and $n$ is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(6m, 6n) = 6 \\cdot \\text{LCM}(m, n)\n\\]\n\nWe know that the LCM of $6m$ and $6n$ is 36, so we can write:\n\n\\[\n36 = 6 \\cdot \\text{LCM}(m, n)\n\\]\n\nSolving for $\\text{LCM}(m, n)$, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(m, n) = \\frac{36}{6} = 6\n\\]\n\nSince $m$ and $n$ are relatively prime, the only possible pairs $(m, n)$ are $(1, 6)$, $(2, 3)$, and $(3, 2)$. The corresponding pairs of the original numbers $(6m, 6n)$ are $(6, 36)$, $(12, 18)$, and $(18, 12)$. We need to check which of these pairs have an LCM of 36.\n\nThe LCM of $6$ and $36$ is 36, because:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(6, 36) = \\frac{6 \\cdot 36}{\\text{GCD}(6, 36)} = \\frac{216}{6} = 36\n\\]\n\nThe LCM of $12$ and $18$ is also 36, because:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(12, 18) = \\frac{12 \\cdot 18}{\\text{GCD}(12, 18)} = \\frac{216}{6} = 36\n\\]\n\nSince both $(6, 36)$ and $(12, 18)$ satisfy the conditions, the two numbers are $6$ and $36$ or $12$ and $18$. In either case, the product of the two numbers is:\n\\[\n6 \\cdot 36 = 216\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{216}\\).",
